        "timestamp": 1774630269,
        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. Why Pakistan has emerged as a mediator between US and Iran Issued on: 27/03/2026 - 17:51 As fears of a wider regional conflict escalate following US and Israeli strikes on Iran that began in late February, Pakistan has emerged as an unexpected mediator, offering to help bring Washington and Tehran to the negotiating table. Islamabad isn't often called on to act as an intermediary in high-stakes diplomacy, but it's stepped into the role this time, both because it has relatively good ties with both Washington and Tehran and because it has a lot at stake in seeing the war resolved. At G7, France hits back at Lavrov, says Russia does not defend international law 27/03/2026 AIDS prevention campaign kicks off in France 27/03/2026 Caribbean: scientists release rare footage of sperm whale birth 27/03/2026 Science US, Israeli war goals 'not realistic': Regime change in Iran 'can't be achieved' by airstrikes 27/03/2026 Middle East North Korea's Kim Jong Un gifted gun by Belarusian leader 27/03/2026 Europe G7 allies press Rubio on US Iran plans, discuss Strait of Hormuz 27/03/2026 Deposed Venezuelan president Maduro makes second NYC court appearance 27/03/2026 South Africa says France withdrew its G7 invitation due to US pressure 27/03/2026 27/03/2026"
